<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Cospaia</h1><p class="st-meta">1440 CE – 1826 CE · capital Cospaia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A tiny Umbrian republic born of a surveying error in 1440 that lived for nearly four centuries largely on tax-free tobacco.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1440 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-eugene-iv">Pope Eugene IV</a> sold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sansepolcro">Borgo Sansepolcro</a> to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence</a>. The new border was set along a stream, but two streams had the same name, and a strip of land about two kilometres long between them was left out of both states. Its few hundred inhabitants declared themselves independent.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Cospaia had no army, no prisons and no written constitution; a council of elders and heads of families settled affairs in the church. Its motto, Perpetua et firma libertas, was carved over the church door. From the 16th century the villagers grew tobacco, which escaped the monopolies and taxes of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Grand Duchy of Tuscany</a>, and the hamlet became a centre for smuggling.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1826 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Tuscany</a> agreed to divide the territory. The inhabitants accepted in return for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coin each and the right to keep growing tobacco.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-cospaia.jpg" alt="Republic of Cospaia" width="320" height="195"><figcaption>Image: Orange Tuesday · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Cospaia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1440 CE – 1826 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cospaia</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Italian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cospaia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1633&amp;focus=republic-of-cospaia" aria-label="Republic of Cospaia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4973 633 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of Cospaia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5293 889L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5293 889h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5293 889h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Cospaia. <a href="/atlas?year=1633&amp;focus=republic-of-cospaia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
